      "t": "Pictograph of a field."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "陳也。樹穀曰田。象四囗。十，阡陌之制也。凡田之屬皆从田。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形) – pictographic representation of a field divided into four sections. See the original version of 甫 and 里.\nSometimes it means mask. See 異 and 鬼. Unrelated to 思, 黄, 果, 由, 甲, and perhaps 番.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *mV-liŋ (“field”). Cognate with Tibetan ཞིང (zhing, “field; realm”), Jingpho maling (mă³¹ liŋ³³, “forest”). Compare Proto-Hmong-Mien *ljiŋ (“field”)."
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "The Min native word for “paddy field; field”.\nEtymology unknown. Chinese scholars identify 塍 (OC *ɦljɯŋ, “raised path between fields”) as the etymological character (本字), although Norman proposes that this is related to 層 (OC *zɯːŋ, “layer”), reflecting the terraced fields commonly found in Fujian (Schuessler, 2007). Compare also 㽪 (“wet field”)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "陳也。樹穀曰田。象四囗。十，阡陌之制也。凡田之屬皆从田。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "exhibit, display. tree corn called field. Depicts 四囗. ten, tenth, footpaths between fields 陌 of system. All characters in the 田 category contain 田 as radical.",
